  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe current 500mb synoptic patten places the CONUS in a meridional \npattern, with a ridge over the eastern half, and a trough digging in \nthe desert southwest. In addition, a high pressure center is placed \nover the Appalachians, with lower pressure developing just east of \nthe Rockies and over the Dakotas. The enhanced pressure gradient is \ncontributing to warm air advection over the central part of the \nCONUS from the Gulf. As a result, our area is currently seeing \ntemperatures close to 70, and wind speeds of 20-30mph, with gusts \naround 35mph. This will continue until sunset, as we lose daytime \nmixing from the solar heating. Winds Saturday will still be gusty, \nbut closer to 15-20mph and gusts to 25mph due to the pressure \ngradient easing and weaker winds at the mixing layer. The \norientation of the jet stream to our west will still allow for one \nmore day of unseasonable temperatures in the upper 60s/low 70s, \nbefore a cold front comes through Saturday night, bringing cooler \ntemperatures and rain.\n\nThe area of surface low pressure progresses northeastward into the \nupper Great Lakes. With addition to the 500mb trough orientation, \nthis will result in a cold front to line up across central Iowa into \ncentral Kansas by Saturday evening. Along this line, showers will \ndevelop, and will become more widespread as they approach our area \nin the late evening and overnight hours. The front will be our \nlifting mechanism, and with high shear, we cannot rule out isolated \ngusty winds and small hail. Severe chances will remain low with lack \nof instability as temperatures at the 700mb and 500mb levels do not \nget cold enough with respect to surface temperatures. To get severe \nweather at this time of year, you need really cold temperatures \naloft for a parcel to rise high enough for instability, and lack of \nthis is often a limiting factor. In terms of precipitation totals, \nhighest amounts will be likely across the NW portions of the CWA, \nwith the NBM guidance suggesting a 50-70% probability of seeing \ntotals greater than 1\". The remaining portions of the CWA are given \na 50-70% probability of seeing around 0.5\" totals. \n\nShower activity lingers around Sunday morning before diminishing and \npushing off to the east with the cold front. As a result, \ntemperatures Sunday and into next week will be influenced by a \nCanadian high pressure system that will develop north of the \nDakotas. This will progress southeastward early next week, and bring \nnortherly winds to accompany colder temperatures. Highs early to mid \nnext week consistently stay in the upper 30-mid 40s, with lows well \nin the 20s. As we get into the middle of next week, ensembles are \nsuggesting a 500mb trough to dig south into the Gulf, with a low \nplaced right overhead. This would further enforce cooler and drier \nconditions for the rest of the forecast period, due to the trough \nblocking warmer air and moisture from the Gulf Coast.",
